// Parameter Deletion Detected (T1485 — Data Destruction)
//
// Deletion is terminal. AWS: "Deleting a parameter removes all versions of it.
// Once deleted, the parameter and its versions can't be restored." No recovery
// window, no soft delete — unlike Secrets Manager, which gives you 7 to 30 days.
// Everything below is written on the assumption that by the time this query
// returns, the data is gone and the only questions left are what and how much.
//
// Counting EVENTS is not counting destruction. DeleteParameters accepts up to ten
// names per call, so the array length is the unit, not the row. The query reads
// requestParameters.names and falls back to requestParameters.name for the
// singular call.
//
// Two blind spots this query cannot close, both documented:
//   * DeleteParameter against a name that does not exist emits NO CloudTrail event
//     at all — AWS states the ParameterNotFound exception "is not recorded in AWS
//     CloudTrail event logs". Probing the namespace through the delete API is
//     invisible.
//   * A parameter removed by an Expiration parameter policy is deleted by
//     Parameter Store itself. No principal, no delete event. That destruction is
//     armed at PutParameter time and is only visible there.
//
// Dialect: Sentinel / Azure Log Analytics KQL. NOT CloudWatch Logs Insights.
AWSCloudTrail
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| where EventSource == "ssm.amazonaws.com"
| where EventName in ("DeleteParameter", "DeleteParameters")
| extend Req  = parse_json(RequestParameters)
| extend Resp = parse_json(ResponseElements)
| extend SingleName = tostring(coalesce(Req.name, Req.Name, ""))
| extend NameArray  = todynamic(coalesce(Req.names, Req.Names, dynamic([])))
| extend Requested  = iff(array_length(NameArray) > 0, NameArray, pack_array(SingleName))
// responseElements presence for DeleteParameters is NOT documented by AWS. When it
// is there, deletedParameters is authoritative; when it is not, fall back to the
// requested list and reconcile against the live inventory (see the note below).
| extend Confirmed  = todynamic(coalesce(Resp.deletedParameters, Resp.DeletedParameters, dynamic([])))
| extend Invalid    = todynamic(coalesce(Resp.invalidParameters, Resp.InvalidParameters, dynamic([])))
| mv-expand RequestedName = Requested to typeof(string)
| where isnotempty(RequestedName)
| summarize
    Calls            = dcount(AwsRequestId),
    NamesRequested   = dcount(RequestedName),
    RequestedNames   = make_set(RequestedName, 100),
    ConfirmedDeleted = make_set_if(tostring(Confirmed), array_length(Confirmed) > 0, 20),
    NotDeleted       = make_set_if(tostring(Invalid), array_length(Invalid) > 0, 20),
    Errors           = make_set_if(ErrorCode, isnotempty(ErrorCode), 10),
    SourceIPs        = make_set(SourceIpAddress, 5),
    Agents           = make_set(UserAgent, 3),
    FirstSeen        = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen         = max(TimeGenerated)
    by UserIdentityArn, bin(TimeGenerated, 15m)
| where not(UserIdentityArn contains "role/iac-deploy" or UserIdentityArn contains "role/ci-cd")
| extend Verdict = case(
    NamesRequested >= 25,
        "MASS PARAMETER DESTRUCTION — unrecoverable, treat as an availability incident now, P0",
    NamesRequested >= 5,
        "MULTIPLE PARAMETERS DESTROYED OUTSIDE THE PIPELINE — unrecoverable, P0",
    "PARAMETER DESTROYED OUTSIDE THE PIPELINE — confirm ownership and consumers, P1")
| project FirstSeen, LastSeen, UserIdentityArn, Calls, NamesRequested, RequestedNames,
          ConfirmedDeleted, NotDeleted, Errors, SourceIPs, Agents, Verdict
| order by NamesRequested desc
//
// No production-path filter is hard-coded above, deliberately: the prefix is
// deployment-specific and one written here would match nothing in most accounts.
// Add your own before the summarize, e.g.
//   | where RequestedName startswith "/app/prod/"
// and raise the verdict for that subset.
//
// ============================================================================
// WHAT WAS ACTUALLY DESTROYED — reconcile, do not assume
// ============================================================================
// A successful DeleteParameters can name ten parameters and delete none of them:
// its only documented error is InternalServerError, and unknown names are returned
// in the 200-response InvalidParameters array. So "no ErrorCode" is not "ten gone".
//
// Reconcile RequestedNames against what still exists:
//   aws ssm describe-parameters \
//     --parameter-filters "Key=Path,Option=Recursive,Values=<path>" \
//     --query 'Parameters[].Name'
// A requested name that is absent now was destroyed. A requested name still
// present was not — either it was in InvalidParameters, or it has been recreated
// since, which is a different and equally important finding.
//
// ============================================================================
// RECOVERY — there is none inside AWS
// ============================================================================
// GetParameterHistory does NOT help: history is deleted with the parameter. The
// value has to come from an external source — the configuration repository, the
// system of record for the secret, or a backup. Recreating the name requires
// waiting: AWS states "after deleting a parameter, wait for at least 30 seconds to
// create a parameter with the same name."
//
// ============================================================================
// ERROR CODES
// ============================================================================
// DeleteParameter: ParameterNotFound (NOT recorded in CloudTrail),
// InternalServerError. DeleteParameters: InternalServerError only. Denials as
// AccessDenied and AccessDeniedException — match both, and confirm against a real
// denied event. There is no ThrottlingException-shaped signal to lean on here:
// deleting is cheap and fast.
